There are three patches I know of:
Ray Chason's Hell Patch (3.4.0) adds new demons and their lairs.
    http://www.smart.net/~rchason/nethack/
Mik's Lethe Patch (3.3.1, but possibly being integrated into SLASH'EM?)
    Very challenging from what I hear, adds new amensia-causing
    water levels, monsters zap wands like cancellation at you.
    http://e-wire.net.au/~eb_mykae/
My own Heck patch (3.4.1) modifies maze drawning algorithms.
    Mostly experimental, doesn't add new special levels.
    http://www.geocities.com/cheepicus
(Others?)

All three require compiling, and you'll notice that none
of them are for the current version. Counting SLASH'EM
with the above, what most people have done is what
you have said: make new levels and substitute them in for
some of the mazes. And because of how NetHack levels live
separately from the main code there are other benefits,
one of which is that you can get pretty far without knowing
C. Other benefits are that if you stick entirely to level
modifications then: save/bones compatibility will certainly
not be affected, your levels should be very version
insensitive, and you can distribute nhdat files instead
of icky patches and bloaty executables. So that approach
has a lot of merit if you should decide to pursue it.

Googling finds very helpful things from Rob Ellwood:
http://members.shaw.ca/rob.ellwood/
specifically:
http://members.shaw.ca/rob.ellwood/MakingNewLevels.txt

Also Dylan's debug mode guide helps once you're
ready to start investigating your own levels:
http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/wizmode.txt

I've been puttering around with a (second) patch using this
approach, and I have a few levels already done. None
of them are exactly bigrooms but there are wide open
spaces.

